  ],
  "text": "A tree is mostly made of air. Almost none of it comes out of the ground. Everyone assumes it ate the soil. It is the obvious answer. So in the sixteen hundreds, a man weighed the soil, planted a willow, and waited five years. The tree gained seventy kilos. The soil had lost about sixty grams. So where did all the wood come from? Water, and the air around it. Leaves pull carbon dioxide out of the sky and lock the carbon into wood. That is what a trunk is. Air, gathered up and made solid. So when a tree stores carbon, that is not a metaphor. It is building itself from it. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
